  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers, and possibly a few thunderstorms, with \npockets of torrential rainfall possible early Sun am into the \nafternoon. A Flood Watch has been issued for RI and southeast MA.  \n\nOne more beautiful day is on tap with plenty of sunshine and high \ntemps this afternoon in the 70s. \n\nOtherwise...the main forecast concern will be an approaching \nshortwave and associated warm from very late tonight into Sunday. As \nthis shortwave approaches...it will result in a very strong \ninstability burst. Showalter indices drop near 20 units to near or \nbelow zero inside 12 hours. That rapid change in stability is often \na good sign for a complex of widespread showers and the potential \nfor a few embedded thunderstorms. In addition...Pwats climb to \naround 2.25 inches across RI/SE MA which is quite high for this part \nof the country and supportive of pockets of torrential rainfall. The \nGEFS situational awareness tables indicate Pwat values outside the \nmodels climatology near the south coast Sunday morning.  \n\nSo in a nutshell...the ingredients above are concerning for a narrow \nband of torrential rainfall and the potential for localized flash \nflooding. The HREF has probabilities for 6-24 HR precipitation \nvalues to exceed 10 year ARI across parts of eastern CT/RI and \nsoutheast MA. There are even very low probabilities to exceed 100 \nyear ARI values near the south coast. That being said...whether or \nnot this actually materializes remains uncertain and will depend on \nthe mesoscale. And on top of that...even if it occurs trying to \ndetermine the specific location is still beyond the state of the \nscience. But we do know this risk will hinge on the potential for \nwhere a mesoscale low tracks...which would setup an area of enhanced \nlow level convergence for the southerly LLJ to impinge upon. \n\nGiven the above...we have opted to issue a Flood Watch very late \ntonight into Sunday for RI and southeast MA. Whether or not this \nnarrow band of torrential rain falls across this region or remains \nto our south is uncertain...but given the potential felt it is just \na Watch and worth highlighting the potential. Widespread rainfall of \n0.50\" to 1.50\" expected across much of our region...But localized \nnarrow bands of 2\"-4\" of rain possible inside 3 hours. If this \noccurs...the potential for localized flash flooding would increase \nespecially if it occurs over a vulnerable urban center. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Any lingering scattered showers depart early \nMon...otherwise dry/seasonable Mon-Tue but a brief bout of summer \nwarmth and humidity possible Wed and/or Thu ahead of a cold front. \n\nThe actual cold front crosses the region later Sunday night into \nearly Monday morning. This may be accompanied by a few brief \nshowers. Otherwise...high pressure builds back into the region Mon \nand Tue bringing dry and seasonable weather. High temps will mainly \nbe in the upper 60s to the middle 70s with lows in the 40s and 50s \nboth days. This high pressure system moves east of the region by the \nmiddle of next week. This may allow for a brief return of summerlike \nwarmth/humidity sometime Wed and/or Thu.",
